# Copy this file:
#   cp .env.example .env        # DATABASE_URL — read by the Prisma CLI and the app
# Secrets for the running app go in .env.local (Next.js loads it; it is gitignored).
# NEVER commit real secrets. .env, .env.local, .env.* are gitignored (this file is the exception).
#
# In production (e.g. Vercel) there are no files — set every value below as an
# environment variable in the hosting dashboard instead. See DEPLOYMENT.md.

# --- Database (required) ---
# PostgreSQL in development and production alike, so local behaviour matches live.
# Get a free database from Neon (neon.tech) or Vercel Postgres — no local install.
#
# Use sslmode=verify-full for a hosted database — NOT the sslmode=require that
# most providers hand you in their copy-paste string.
#
# The pg driver currently treats "require", "prefer" and "verify-ca" as aliases
# for "verify-full", and warns loudly that it will stop doing so: in pg v9 they
# adopt libpq semantics, where "require" encrypts the connection but does NOT
# verify the server's certificate. That is a downgrade to an attacker-in-the-
# middle, and it will arrive as a silent behaviour change on a dependency bump.
# Asking for verify-full explicitly keeps today's behaviour permanently and
# silences the warning.
#
# A LOCAL database over a unix socket or loopback needs no TLS at all — leave
# the parameter off entirely rather than setting it to something weaker.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST/DBNAME?sslmode=verify-full"
# Optional. Connections per instance; keep small on serverless. Default 5.
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=""

# --- Auth (required) — put these in .env.local locally ---
# Session signing secret. Generate one with:  npx auth secret   (or: openssl rand -base64 32)
# Use a DIFFERENT value in production than in development.
AUTH_SECRET=""
# Trust the host header when self-hosting / running locally. Not needed on Vercel.
AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true

# --- Who may create an account ---
# Anyone. Registration is open by design.
# What keeps that from costing you is the free plan: it does not include Deep
# Reviews or plans projections, the two runs that call the model. See FREE_QUOTA
# in lib/billing/quota.ts. To close signup entirely, put the deployment behind
# your host's password/SSO protection rather than the app.

# --- Anthropic API (Milestone 5) — SERVER-SIDE ONLY, never exposed to the client ---
# Get a key at https://console.anthropic.com
# WITHOUT a key the app still works: it produces a clearly-labelled SAMPLE
# evaluation instead of calling the model, so you can try the whole flow first.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""
# Optional. Defaults to claude-opus-5 (verified against the current model list).
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=""
# Optional. low | medium | high | xhigh | max. Defaults to "medium" —
# raise for more thorough (and more expensive) evaluations.
ANTHROPIC_EFFORT=""
# --- Follow-up evaluations (the main cost lever) ---
# After the first evaluation of a profile, later runs are FOLLOW-UPS: the
# previous scores are fed back in as an anchor, unchanged items carry their
# assessments forward, and the model's job is "what did this change" rather than
# "judge this from nothing". That is a smaller task, so it runs on a cheaper
# model by default.
#
# The anchor is what makes this safe rather than a way of quietly swapping the
# judge: an anchored run reproduces a calibration the strong model already set.
# So it is used ONLY where the anchor is intact. A first run, or one where a
# prompt version redefined a score and released its anchor, goes back to the
# full model automatically. You can also ask for a full run any time from the
# evaluations page.
#
# Set to "off" to run every evaluation on ANTHROPIC_MODEL.
ANTHROPIC_FOLLOWUP_MODEL=""
# Optional. Defaults to the same effort as a baseline run, on purpose: if the
# model AND the effort both changed, an unexplained score movement could not be
# attributed to either.
ANTHROPIC_FOLLOWUP_EFFORT=""

# Optional. 1h | 5m | off. Defaults to "1h".
# Roughly 89% of an evaluation's input is byte-identical every run (the system
# prompt and the rubrics), so it is cached. Cache READS cost 0.1x, but WRITES
# cost 2x at "1h" and 1.25x at "5m" — so this is a bet on how you use the app:
#   1h  — you read a result, edit your profile, run again later. Pays from the
#         third run within an hour. This is the default.
#   5m  — you re-run within a few minutes. Pays from the second run.
#   off — you run once and stop. Avoids paying the write premium for nothing.
ANTHROPIC_CACHE_TTL=""

# --- Projections ("what if I did these things") — optional ---
# Projections run on a CHEAPER model than evaluations on purpose: they reason
# forward from scores an evaluation already established, and they're the run a
# student repeats most while experimenting with plans.
# Defaults to claude-sonnet-5 at "low" effort.
ANTHROPIC_PROJECTION_MODEL=""
ANTHROPIC_PROJECTION_EFFORT=""

# --- How long a run may take (optional) ---
# The routes declare maxDuration = 300 seconds, because an evaluation of a full
# profile does not fit in the 60 that some plans default to. That number is a
# literal the host reads from the build output — it cannot come from here.
#
# Your host CAPS it at whatever your plan allows, and that cap is not visible to
# the app. So if runs are still being killed, set this to the ceiling your plan
# actually enforces: the app then stops itself just before that and records a
# run that says why, instead of being killed mid-stream and surfacing five
# minutes later as an unexplained failure.
EVAL_TIME_BUDGET_SECONDS=""
PROJECTION_TIME_BUDGET_SECONDS=""

# --- Evaluation rate limits (optional) ---
# Per user. Defaults: 20 second cooldown, 10 billable evaluations per hour.
EVAL_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=""
EVAL_MAX_PER_HOUR=""
# Projections have their own budget so plan-tinkering can't lock you out of the
# evaluation that matters. Defaults: 10 second cooldown, 20 per hour.
PROJECTION_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=""
PROJECTION_MAX_PER_HOUR=""

# --- Tests (optional; see TESTING.md) ---
# A SEPARATE throwaway Postgres database for integration and e2e tests. Tests
# WIPE this database, so its name must contain "test" (enforced) and it must
# never be your real one. Leave empty to skip the database-backed tests.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=""

# --- Login brute-force protection (optional) ---
# Consecutive failed logins before the account locks, and for how long.
# Defaults: 10 attempts, 15 minute lockout. A password reset clears the lock,
# so a locked-out user does not have to wait it out.
MAX_FAILED_LOGINS=""
LOGIN_LOCKOUT_MINUTES=""

# --- Password reset (optional) ---
# How long a reset link works for. Default 60 minutes. Keep it short: the link
# is delivered by a human forwarding a URL, and the copy left behind in that
# conversation stays valid for exactly this long.
PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_MINUTES=""
#
# Shown on /forgot-password as the address to ask for a link. Leave empty and
# the page just says to contact whoever runs the instance.
#
# There is no email provider wired up, deliberately — sending to an address you
# do not control needs a verified sending domain, and without one the mail
# fails or lands in spam, which is worse than no button. Mint links yourself:
#
#   DATABASE_URL="<prod>" npx tsx scripts/reset-link.ts them@example.com \
#     --base https://your-app.vercel.app
#
PASSWORD_RESET_CONTACT=""
#
# Used by scripts/reset-link.ts to build the link. Falls back to AUTH_URL,
# NEXTAUTH_URL or VERCEL_URL; the script refuses to guess if none is set,
# because a link to the wrong host looks right and goes nowhere.
APP_URL=""


# --- Payments (optional; see DEPLOYMENT.md step 5) ---
# LEAVE THESE EMPTY AND THE APP RUNS EXACTLY AS IT DID BEFORE BILLING EXISTED:
# everybody is on the free plan, no checkout button is rendered anywhere, and
# the webhook endpoint returns 503. Nothing is hidden, disabled or degraded.
#
# Test mode and live mode have SEPARATE keys, price ids and webhook secrets.
# A test price id in a live deployment fails at checkout.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""
#
# From Stripe → Developers → Webhooks. The webhook is the ONLY thing that grants
# a plan — the page a customer lands on after paying is just a redirect, and
# anyone can type it, so it grants nothing.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
#
# The recurring PRICE ids (price_...), not the product ids (prod_...). Confusing
# the two is the most common setup mistake and shows up as checkout returning
# 400. A plan whose id is unset simply cannot be bought; it is not an error.
STRIPE_PRICE_STUDENT_PLUS=""
STRIPE_PRICE_TUTOR_20=""
STRIPE_PRICE_TUTOR_50=""
#
# What the app DISPLAYS, in whole dollars. Stripe holds the amount actually
# charged, so if you price something differently there, set it here too or the
# app quotes a price it does not charge.
STUDENT_PLUS_PRICE_USD=""
TUTOR_20_PRICE_USD=""
TUTOR_50_PRICE_USD=""

# --- Data retention (optional) ---
# The written parts of old evaluations are deleted on a schedule, so this
# deployment holds less about its students over time. You cannot leak what you
# no longer have, and this is the cheap version of that — no keys, nothing a
# user can lose.
#
# SCORES ARE NEVER DELETED BY THIS. The progress chart is meant to cover four
# years of secondary school, so the numbers are denormalized out of the write-up
# (Evaluation.chartPointJson) and outlive it. Only prose expires.
#
# Days before the profile SNAPSHOT taken at each evaluation is removed. Default
# 60. This is the raw profile — essay drafts, activity write-ups — and its only
# consumer is the diff between two nearby runs.
RETENTION_INPUT_SNAPSHOT_DAYS=""
#
# Days before the evaluation WRITE-UP is removed. Default 365, so a student can
# re-read across a whole application cycle and an autumn follow-up still anchors
# to the previous autumn.
RETENTION_RESULT_DAYS=""
#
# Set either to 0 to keep that field forever. A malformed value falls back to
# the default rather than to "never" — an unparseable number should not switch
# a retention guarantee off silently.
#
# The sweep runs weekly from vercel.json and authenticates with CRON_SECRET,
# the same variable the counselor triage job uses. Without it the endpoint
# rejects every request, so nothing is ever deleted.

# --- Plan quotas (optional) ---
# What each plan is SOLD as, enforced as minimum intervals between runs:
# the paid tier is one Deep Review every 30 days, a plans projection every 7,
# and a check-in every 2 — matching the Stripe product description. The free
# tier is deliberately less (90 / 30 / 14).
#
# These live in lib/billing/quota.ts rather than here, because they are a
# PROMISE MADE ON A PRICING PAGE, not a deployment knob — changing one without
# changing the Stripe product description means the app stops honouring what a
# customer bought. A test asserts the paid intervals match the advertised ones.
#
# Access codes grant a single run past the quota, for handing out during
# testing. Mint them with:
#   DATABASE_URL="<prod>" npx tsx scripts/make-access-code.ts --kind DEEP_REVIEW
# A code is spent only when the interval would otherwise refuse, so handing one
# out early never wastes it.
